# Health Checks & Quick Curls

Once `make all` finishes, a few commands tell you whether every front door is
actually answering.

## The two health targets

```bash
make healthcheck          # curls each front door + the BaaS health route
make check_health_apps    # UP/DOWN matrix per app, localhost AND production
```

- `make healthcheck` probes the BaaS auth health route, the bridge, osionos, the
  website (following the HTTP→HTTPS redirect), and the auth gateway's availability
  endpoint.
- `make check_health_apps` produces a per-app UP/DOWN table across **both**
  localhost and the deployed production URLs.

## Poking a single surface

The local TLS certificates are self-signed by a local CA, so pass that CA to
`curl`:

```bash
# Website
curl --cacert apps/grobase/certs/track-binocle-local-ca.pem https://localhost:4322

# osionos editor
curl --cacert apps/grobase/certs/track-binocle-local-ca.pem https://localhost:3001
```

> [!warning] The auth gateway has no /health route
> Probe its availability endpoint instead:
> `https://localhost:8787/api/auth/availability`. A 404 on `/health` is expected,
> not a failure.

## Everyday inspection

| Command | Use it to… |
|:--------|:-----------|
| `docker compose ps` | See service status at a glance. |
| `docker compose logs -f <service>` | Follow one service's logs. |
| `make showcase` | Reprint the local URLs. |

## Quick green-light checklist

- [ ] `make healthcheck` returns without errors
- [ ] `docker compose ps` shows services healthy (a few vestigial ones may lag)
- [ ] `https://localhost:4322` serves the website
- [ ] `https://localhost:8787/api/auth/availability` answers

> [!note] Production is a separate surface
> `make check_health_apps` also probes the deployed frontends and backend. Those
> live independently of your local Docker stack; a red production row doesn't mean
> your local run is broken. If something local is down, head to
> [Common Failure Modes](common-failure-modes.xmd).
